{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes: register the branch redirect boundary\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a pending redirect valid bit and target register in `rtl/core.sv`.\n  EX redirects are captured only when EX can advance (`!stall_ex_mem` and\n  `!ex_div_busy`), then IF applies the stored target on the following cycle.\n- Routed the pending bit to the hazard unit and IF. IF gives it priority over\n  fetch stalls and emits a bubble while changing to the target.\n- Added a pending redirect squash input to EX. During the apply cycle, EX\n  suppresses redirect generation, divider start, and EX/MEM capture of the\n  younger instruction. Hazard control flushes ID/EX even if an older MEM\n  operation is stalled.\n- Kept EX misalignment detection and its trap/linear-PC behavior unchanged.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed registered boundary. A control transfer\nnow incurs one extra bubble. Gowin timing was not run locally, so the Fmax and\nCoreMark tradeoff remains for the evaluator to measure.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: passed with the requested command.\n- Formal: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures (`RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl\n  CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`).\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` initially used the build script's default NRET=2,\n  which does not match this core's `nret: 1` ports. Re-running as\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed both ELF programs.\n- Gowin timing: place option 0 measured 49.593 MHz with 14 logic levels.\n  Its worst path is register file -> ID -> EX/ALU -> data-memory output.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
