{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && sed -n '1,80p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n# EX-trained return address stack prediction\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added an eight-entry circular return-address stack in `if_stage.sv`.\n- Fetch recognizes canonical `jalr x0, 0(x1)` and `jalr x0, 0(x5)` encodings. With a nonempty stack, it predicts the top entry and advances fetch to that address.\n- Carried `predicted_next_pc` through the IF/ID bundle and ID/EX register.\n- EX compares a canonical return's resolved target with its carried prediction and suppresses a correctly predicted return redirect. Other taken branches and jumps retain their existing redirect behavior. EX pushes `pc+4` for valid JAL/JALR calls writing x1 or x5 and pops valid JALR returns reading x1 or x5 into x0. Updates occur only when the instruction advances through EX.\n- A misaligned predicted return redirects fetch back to `pc+4`, matching the trap's architectural next PC.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\n- The RAS has eight entries; on overflow it overwrites the oldest entry. A stale or overwritten prediction is corrected by EX, but can still cause a flush.\n- Return prediction checks the standard zero-immediate encoding. EX's pop classification follows the specified link-register and destination-register pattern, so noncanonical JALRs may change stack state without receiving a fetch prediction.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: passed.\n- Formal: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures. The initial run exposed missing recovery for a misaligned predicted return; the recovery redirect was added before the passing run.\n- Cosimulation: passed, 2 ELFs tested. `NRET=1` was set to match `cores/bench/core.yaml`; the default `NRET