f here, so keep it only when measured timing gains outweigh datapath overhead.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -0.49%): For a single-issue CoreMark core, a synchronous register-file read plus IF/ID\n…[1477 of 4549 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:71c1d50bd1a3ef38]…\nrtials, and MEM selection add datapath cost, so narrower address adders need measured timing gains to pay off.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r7s2 (regression, -0.29%): Moving canonical `JALR` return redirects into ID still regressed slightly because return decoding, target selection, and alignment checks add work to the ID redirect path, while the EX bubbles saved were too few to offset that cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r8s1 (regression, -3.55%): Moving load/store address generation into ID can lose on CoreMark when stalls behind matching ID/EX writers and EX/MEM loads add bubbles while the forwarded base-plus-immediate adder becomes ID-stage work; EX path relief must cover both costs.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r8s2 (regression, -0.26%): Even when direct JAL reuses ID's PC-relative adder, its early redirect and `jal_early_redirect` pipeline suppression logic did not repay the sequential wrong-path redirect saved on CoreMark, so measure the dynamic call benefit before extending ID redirect control.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
