{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg --files cores/bench | sort | rg '(result|metric|log|csv|json|report|LESSONS|rtl/(if_stage|ex_stage|core_pkg|id_stage|core)\\\\.sv)'\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +759.65%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a radix-2 EX unit removes the divider from the forwarding-to-data-address critical path, and CoreMark's rare divides make the 32-cycle latency affordable.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 (regression, -5.43%): A resetless asynchronous 2R register array with per-entry valid bits can worsen timing because validity gating adds selection to both read paths; LUT-RAM inference must outweigh that added read-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.79%): In this five-stage CoreMark core, the fetch-time return decode and variable-indexed 8×32 RAS lookup cost more than the EX-resolved return redirects they saved, so RAS depth and lookup logic should track measured canonical-return hit rates.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, -6.29%): Explicit one-hot decode, per-bit masking, and 32-word OR reductions can map worse than variable-index register-file reads, so compare mapped timing and area before replacing inferred muxes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.09%): Registering EX redirects breaks the same-cycle feedback path but adds a squash bubble per taken branch or jump; CoreMark's frequent calls and returns make that throughput cost outweigh the timing benefit.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.37%): A four-entry fully associative BTB's parallel tag compares, target/counter storage, and carried predicted-PC state regressed CoreMark fitness despite EX redirect suppression, so weigh fetch-table cost against redirects actually saved.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, +0.16%): A p