{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260930-002-r13s1\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260930-001-r13s0\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20260930-001-r11s0\\\") | {id,title,motivation,hypothesis,critical_path,implementation_notes}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg --files cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | sort | tail -20 && git status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260930-001-r11s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Precompute EX forwarding selects in ID\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst timing path runs from a MEM/WB destination through the forwarding comparisons and EX operand muxes into the ALU and data-memory address. The full-width result path is short enough that the select logic is now a meaningful part of the critical path. Moving the register comparisons across the ID/EX boundary should leave only a registered select and operand mux in EX.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst timing path runs from a MEM/WB destination through the forwarding comparisons and EX operand muxes into the ALU and data-memory address. The full-width result path is short enough that the select logic is now a meaningful part of the critical path. Moving the register comparisons across the ID/EX boundary should leave only a registered select and operand mux in EX.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Compute the forwarding priority for the instruction being decoded, using the current ID/EX and EX/MEM writers, then capture the select codes alongside that instruction in ID/EX. Because those producers advance to EX/MEM and MEM/WB on the same edge, encode their next-cycle source positions when precomputing the selects. The load-use interlock already delays a consumer until a load reaches MEM/WB, and pipeline stalls hold the instruction and its selectors together. EX can then use the registered selects without the late destination-tag comparisons, shortening the forwarding-to-ALU/address path without adding an adder or changing instruction latency.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 7,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Compare the incoming source registers against current ID/EX and EX/MEM writers, encode the matching producers' next-cycle EX/MEM or MEM/WB positions, and capture the selects with each ID/EX instruction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n        \"\n…[3761 of 6833 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:23f13000bec7962c]…\n redirect target, ID redirect target, sequential PC+4, and held PC. Keep reset behavior, EX-over-ID priority, both redirects' priority over stalls, and the same branch/redirect timing. A shallow parallel selection cone should reduce the control-to-PC setup path while retaining the cycle count of the existing ID-resolved branch pipeline.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 6,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Flatten the PC register's redirect, sequential-advance, and hold arbitration into parallel source selection while preserving current priorities.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.alu_op_2_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_if/pc_24_s1\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -13.229,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 23.524,\n    \"logic_cells\": 48,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\",\n      \"cpu/u_rf\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
