{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r3s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-001-r3s0\ntitle: \\\"Shared one-hot register file reads\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's worst path now starts in the register file and ends at an ID/EX operand register. The current two asynchronous reads use variable-index array selection followed by write-first bypass muxes. A previous LUT-RAM attempt worsened timing because it added validity gating, so this proposal keeps the existing storage and architectural behavior while targeting the read-selection logic itself.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decode each read address once into a one-hot select and form the register-file read value with parallel masked-word reduction, retaining x0 behavior and the same-cycle write-first bypass. This may map the wide asynchronous reads into shallower FPGA LUT logic than the inferred binary-index mux trees, shortening the measured register-file-to-ID/EX path without adding pipeline state or changing stalls and forwarding. Keep the implementation limited to the read network so synthesis can compare the alternate mux topology directly.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \\\"Replace variable-index asynchronous read selection with one-hot decoded masked-word reductions; preserve reset, x0, and write-first bypass semantics.\\\"\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
