ption\": \"Add EX divide-busy stalling so younger instructions remain held until the divide result can advance, without repeatedly retiring older stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the EX divide-busy handshake to fetch/decode hazard control and preserve the existing m\n…[28054 of 31126 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e0db9449e988fb0b]…\nX.\\n- Concern: the BTB adds tag/target storage and fetch comparison logic. Local Gowin timing reported 34.269 MHz (26 logic levels); the worst path starts at a register-file output and ends at ID/EX capture. Area was 3268 logic cells and 937 registers. This may limit the fitness gain even if the BTB reduces redirect bubbles.\\n- Local checks: Verilator lint passed; formal passed (53 passed, 0 failed); cosim passed for both selftest and CoreMark (`NRET=1`, including the configured stall cases). The first cosim invocation used its default `NRET=2` and failed to compile against the core's declared `nret: 1`; rerunning with `NRET=1` passed.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T14:24:34.022551Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.37%): A four-entry fully associative BTB's parallel tag compares, target/counter storage, and carried predicted-PC state regressed CoreMark fitness despite EX redirect suppression, so weigh fetch-table cost against redirects actually saved.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
