{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +759.65%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a radix-2 EX unit removes the divider from the forwarding-to-data-address critical path, and CoreMark's rare divides make the 32-cycle latency affordable.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 (regression, -5.43%): A resetless asynchronous 2R register array with per-entry valid bits can worsen timing because validity gating adds selection to both read paths; LUT-RAM inference must outweigh that added read-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.79%): In this five-stage CoreMark core, the fetch-time return decode and variable-indexed 8×32 RAS lookup cost more than the EX-resolved return redirects they saved, so RAS depth and lookup logic should track measured canonical-return hit rates.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, -6.29%): Explicit one-hot decode, per-bit masking, and 32-word OR reductions can map worse than variable-index register-file reads, so compare mapped timing and area before replacing inferred muxes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.09%): Registering EX redirects breaks the same-cycle feedback path but adds a squash bubble per taken branch or jump; CoreMark's frequent calls and returns make that throughput cost outweigh the timing benefit.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.37%): A four-entry fully associative BTB's parallel tag compares, target/counter storage, and carried predicted-PC state regressed CoreMark fitness despite EX redirect suppression, so weigh fetch-table cost against redirects actually saved.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, +0.16%): A parallel `rs1 + imm` effective-address path duplicates the 32-bit sum and adds an EX result mux; bypassing ALU selectors alone did not pay off here, so keep it only when measured timing gains outweigh datapath overhead.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -0.49%): For a single-issue CoreMark core, a synchronous register-file read plus IF/ID stage needs measured timing gains large enough to offset added pipeline-fill and redirect latency; this version regressed slightly.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r4s2 (regression, -2.52%): Carrying one prediction bit saves PC metadata, but `imm_gen` plus `pc + immediate` and prediction selection in IF puts target formation on the fetch PC path; that cost outweighed saved redirects on CoreMark.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r5s0 (regression, -3.37%): Sharing ALU comparison flags with branch resolution lengthens the forwarded-operand redirect path when flags are computed after the ALU's `is_auipc`/`alu_src` operand muxes, so comparator sharing must preserve the branch's direct `rs1`/`rs2` inputs to help timing.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r5s1 (improvement, +8.85%): Resolving conditional branches in ID shortens the EX-to-fetch redirect path without adding the squash bubble of registered EX redirects, but requires ID operand bypasses and interlocks for in-flight writers and loads.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r5s2 (regression, -9.21%): A two-entry explicit-register RAS still regressed CoreMark because canonical-return decode, a 32-bit fetch target select, and carried target/EX-match metadata remain front-end costs; removing the indexed stack mux alone does not pay for the redirects saved.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
