{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r6s0.yaml && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-001-r6s0\ntitle: \"One-hot EX forwarding selects\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's worst path starts at the MEM/WB destination register and runs through forwarding and EX into the ALU before reaching the data-memory address. Forwarding currently turns destination matches into a two-bit priority code, then decodes that code in EX to select among three operands. That encode-and-decode sequence sits directly on the measured path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Generate mutually exclusive one-hot selects for the ID/EX value, EX/MEM value, and MEM/WB value for each source register, preserving EX/MEM priority and x0 behavior. Replace the two-bit forwarding codes and case muxes with a masked-OR operand mux in EX. Removing select re-decoding should shorten the forwarding-control path into the ALU and improve Fmax while leaving instruction timing and forwarding semantics unchanged; the mux is small enough that its mapping cost may stay below the larger register-file one-hot experiment.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Produce one-hot none, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB selects per source with explicit youngest-writer priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the one-hot forwarding selects in masked-OR operand muxes instead of decoding two-bit select codes.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect the one-hot forwarding control vectors between the forwarding unit and EX stage.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
