{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"ls -l cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml; git check-ignore -v cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml || true; sed -n '1,80p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"-rw-rw----+ 1 hwebench2 hwebench2 1333 Sep 30 17:37 cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml\n.gitignore:88:cores/*/experiments/hypotheses/\tcores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r7s0.yaml\nid: hyp-20260930-001-r7s0\ntitle: \"Use a byte-group carry-select ALU adder\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's measured critical path starts in MEM/WB forwarding and ends at the data-memory address, passing through EX arithmetic. Effective addresses use the ALU's ordinary 32-bit addition, so carry propagation after the forwarding mux is directly on this path. Prior address splitting moved work across the EX/MEM boundary but added fields and MEM selection; a local carry-select adder can shorten the sum without changing pipeline state or latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the ALU_ADD ripple-style 32-bit sum with a four-byte carry-select structure. Compute the low byte and its carry, compute both carry-in versions of each upper byte in parallel, and select each byte sum and outgoing carry using the preceding block's carry. This keeps the architectural result and cycle timing unchanged while limiting serial carry propagation to byte-sized additions and carry-select muxes, which should improve the forwarding-to-effective-address path and reduce Fmax pressure on other ADD users.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Implement ALU_ADD with four 8-bit carry-select groups while preserving all other operation results and ALTOPS behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
