{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"find cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses -maxdepth 1 -type f -printf '%f\\\\n' && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r9s0.yaml && git check-ignore -v cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r9s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260930-001-r9s0.yaml\nid: hyp-20260930-001-r9s0\ntitle: \"Select forwarded address after parallel sums\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current worst path runs from a MEM/WB register through forwarding,\n  EX operand selection, and the ALU to the data-memory address. Recent\n  forwarding-select and ALU-result bypass experiments did not materially\n  shorten that path. The forwarding decision is independent of address\n  arithmetic, so it can be resolved in parallel with candidate sums.\nhypothesis: |\n  For load and store instructions, compute rs1-plus-immediate in parallel\n  for the ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB rs1 candidates, then select the completed\n  sum using the existing forwarding select. Register that selected sum as\n  the effective address while retaining the general ALU result for other\n  instructions. This moves the forwarding comparison and operand mux off\n  the serial forwarding-to-address-adder path, trading additional adders\n  for a shorter path without changing instruction latency or dependencies.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Speculatively add the memory immediate to each rs1 forwarding source and select the completed effective address after the parallel adders.\"\n.gitignore:88:cores/*/experiments/hypotheses/\tcores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r9s0.yaml\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
