{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +759.65%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a radix-2 EX unit removes the divider from the forwarding-to-data-address critical path, and CoreMark's rare divides make the 32-cycle latency affordable.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 (regression, -5.43%): A resetless asynchronous 2R register array with per-entry valid bits can worsen timing because validity gating adds selection to both read paths; LUT-RAM inference must outweigh that added read-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, -2.79%): In this five-stage CoreMark core, the fetch-time return decode and variable-indexed 8×32 RAS lookup cost more than the EX-resolved return redirects they saved, so RAS depth and lookup logic should track measured canonical-return hit rates.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, -6.29%): Explicit one-hot decode, per-bit masking, and 32-word OR reductions can map worse than variable-index register-file reads, so compare mapped timing and area before replacing inferred muxes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.09%): Registering EX redirects breaks the same-cycle feedback path but adds a squash bubble per taken branch or jump; CoreMark's frequent calls and returns make that throughput cost outweigh the timing benefit.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -20.37%): A four-entry fully associative BTB's parallel tag compares, target/counter storage, and carried predicted-PC state regressed CoreMark fitness despite EX redirect suppression, so weigh fetch-table cost against redirects actually saved.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, +0.16%): A parallel `rs1 + imm` effective-address path duplicates the 32-bit sum and adds an EX result mux; bypassing ALU selectors alone did not pay off here, so keep it only when measured timing gains outweigh datapath overhead.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -0.49%): For a single-issue CoreMark core, a synchronous register-file read plus IF/ID\n…[2118 of 5190 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c162e920afd19717]…\nbecomes ID-stage work; EX path relief must cover both costs.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r8s2 (regression, -0.26%): Even when direct JAL reuses ID's PC-relative adder, its early redirect and `jal_early_redirect` pipeline suppression logic did not repay the sequential wrong-path redirect saved on CoreMark, so measure the dynamic call benefit before extending ID redirect control.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r9s1 (regression, -28.30%): A one-entry `mem_wb_t` skid with completion muxing and `pipeline_block`/redirect control regressed despite dmem stalls, showing that hiding at most one younger ALU instruction per request must justify the added register and control cost on CoreMark.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r9s2 (regression, +3.19%): Even a one-entry return target cache keeps canonical-return decode, a fetch tag compare/target select, and 33 bits of carried prediction state in the front end, while suppressing EX redirects only on hits, so its always-paid cost still outweighed saved redirects on CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
