{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('id') in ('hyp-20260930-002-r7s1','hyp-20260930-002-r6s1','hyp-20260930-002-r5s1'):\n  print('\\\\n###',r['id'],r.get('title'),r.get('outcome'))\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('error:',r.get('error'))\n  print('notes:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n### hyp-20260930-002-r5s1 Resolve conditional branches in decode improvement\nCompute conditional branch comparisons and PC-relative targets in ID, then carry the resolved taken bit, target, and alignment result with the instruction into EX/MEM for RVFI reporting. Add EX/MEM and MEM/WB operand bypasses for ID branch comparisons, and hold fetch while a matching older writer is still in ID/EX or an older load has not reached MEM/WB. Redirect fetch as soon as an aligned branch is resolved in ID while still allowing that branch itself to enter ID/EX; keep JAL and JALR resolution in EX. Registering the branch outcome before EX removes the register-file-to-EX-to-fetch control chain and should improve Fmax while saving a cycle on taken branches.\n\nerror: None\nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 Split effective address generation across EX and MEM regression\nFor loads and stores, divide effective-address generation into low and high 16-bit portions across the existing EX/MEM register. In EX, use the forwarded base register and immediate to capture the low-half sum and carry, plus high-half sums precomputed for both carry values. In MEM, select the appropriate high half and assemble the address for the existing dmem request. Keep the normal ALU result path for non-memory instructions, and preserve the dmem stall behavior by holding the partial address fields with EX/MEM. This replaces the forwarding-to-dmem 32-bit adder path with parallel half-width sums followed by a mux, while preserving same-cycle memory handshakes.\n\nerror: None\nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20260930-002-r7s1 Split operand forwarding from execute broken\nSplit the execute stage into an operand stage and an ALU/result stage. The operand stage resolves source values with the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB bypass priorities and captures operands, immediate, and controls; the following stage performs the ALU operation and registers the result for MEM. Add a bypass from the immediately older ALU result into the operand-stage capture, and extend load-use interlocks so a consumer waits until a load value is available. Preserve single-instruction-per-cycle flow, dmem stall behavior, ID-resolved conditional branches, RVFI ordering, and correct squash/redirect handling across the added boundary. This separates the forwarding comparators/muxes from the wide ALU operation and should improve Fmax enough to offset the added control-transfer and load-use latency.\n\nerror: cosim_failed: random2.elf [--istall --dstall]\nnotes: None\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
