{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\") if x.strip()];\nfor wanted in [\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r6s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r9s1\\\"]:\n r=next(x for x in rows if x.get(\\\"id\\\")==wanted); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",wanted); print(r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"motivation\\\")); print(r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\")); print(\\\"path\\\",r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\")); print(\\\"notes\\\",r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\"))' \"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260930-002-r6s1\nThe current best already resolves conditional branches in decode, and its remaining measured critical path runs from MEM/WB forwarding through EX arithmetic to the data-memory address output. A full-width address sum is still on that memory request path, so splitting the sum at the existing EX/MEM boundary may reduce its combinational depth without inserting a pipeline bubble.\n\nFor loads and stores, divide effective-address generation into low and high 16-bit portions across the existing EX/MEM register. In EX, use the forwarded base register and immediate to capture the low-half sum and carry, plus high-half sums precomputed for both carry values. In MEM, select the appropriate high half and assemble the address for the existing dmem request. Keep the normal ALU result path for non-memory instructions, and preserve the dmem stall behavior by holding the partial address fields with EX/MEM. This replaces the forwarding-to-dmem 32-bit adder path with parallel half-width sums followed by a mux, while preserving same-cycle memory handshakes.\n\npath {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.alu_op_3_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/pc_9_s1', 'slack_ns': -14.088, 'arrival_ns': 24.382, 'logic_cells': 35, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\nThe best design's measured critical path runs from a forwarded register value through the EX operand mux and ALU to the data-memory address. The prior EX/MEM carry-select split shortened that path by only 3.32% and added partial-sum registers and a MEM mux. Moving the memory address calculation to the existing decode stage removes forwarding and the full-width addition from the memory-request path altogether.\n\nCompute load and store effective addresses in ID from the decoded immediate and the existing register-file/bypass values, then carry the address through ID/EX and EX/MEM for the MEM request, alignment checks, and RVFI memory metadata. Add ID interlocks for a matching writer still in ID/EX and for a matching load in EX/MEM, so the address uses the current value from the existing EX/MEM or MEM/WB bypass. This structurally separates memory address generation from EX forwarding and arithmetic without adding a pipeline stage; the added dependency bubbles apply only when a memory base is not yet available.\n\npath {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.alu_op_1_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/pc_3_s1', 'slack_ns': -14.601, 'arrival_ns': 24.895, 'logic_cells': 43, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260930-002-r9s1\nThe current memory backpressure policy freezes the whole pipeline whenever an EX/MEM load or store sees io_dmemReady low. Those cycles prevent even independent ALU work from advancing, although the single-issue pipeline can safely compute a younger instruction while the older memory request waits.\n\nAdd a one-entry in-order completion skid buffer around the MEM/WB boundary. When a data request stalls, retain its request and architectural record, allow one younger independent non-memory instruction to execute into the buffer, and then stop issuing until the request completes. Keep retirement in program order: retire the memory operation only when its bus handshake completes, then retire the buffered instruction. Preserve load-use interlocks for any younger instruction that reads the outstanding load destination, and keep memory requests ordered. This overlaps short dmem stalls with useful execution without widening retirement or changing the RVFI contract.\n\npath {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.read_data_29_s0', 'slack_ns': -22.81, 'arrival_ns': 33.104, 'logic_cells': 25, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_mem']}\nnotes \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
