{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r11s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r11s1\ntitle: Predecode EX forwarding selectors\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The best design resolves conditional branches in decode, and its measured critical path still runs from the MEM/WB destination tag through the forwarding unit and EX ALU to the data-memory address. The current EX cycle performs register comparisons, forwarding priority selection, operand muxing, and address addition in series.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute each instruction's forwarding source selectors while it is in IF/ID, comparing its source registers against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations. Capture those selectors in ID/EX, with the ID/EX producer taking priority and mapping to next cycle's EX/MEM source, and the EX/MEM producer mapping to next cycle's MEM/WB source. Keep the existing forwarding data muxes and load-use interlock, but remove tag comparison and priority logic from the EX-to-memory address path. This moves small comparisons before the EX cycle without adding a pipeline stage or bubbles; the captured source identities remain aligned with their producers under the existing pipeline stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add the two captured forwarding selectors to the ID/EX payload.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Precompute forwarding priority for the entering instruction from the current ID/EX and EX/MEM producer tags, then capture the selectors with the instruction.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Feed captured selectors to EX and remove the cycle-critical combinational forwarding comparisons from the top-level path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
