off here, so keep it only when measured timing gains outweigh datapath overhead.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -0.49%): For a single-issue CoreMark core, a synchronous register-file read plus IF/ID\n…[5017 of 8089 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d9320ab9d07278c2]…\nt branches moves the forwarded compare onto the EX-to-fetch redirect path and delays taken-branch recovery; on CoreMark those costs outweighed removing the EX/MEM bypass from ID resolution.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r13s0 (regression, -4.50%): A shared 31-bit unsigned branch compare still leaves sign-difference arbitration and branch-op selection on the ID redirect path, so comparator sharing needs mapped timing evidence to justify its added selection logic.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r13s2 (regression, +2.84%): Doubling the direction table to 16 PC[5:2]-indexed counters still regressed CoreMark, so added capacity alone did not make fetch-time predictions accurate enough to repay EX-stage correction redirects on misses.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r14s1 (regression, +4.28%): Capturing older EX/MEM writeback data in ID removed the MEM/WB-to-EX bypass but shifted the critical path to imem-stall feedback through IF, RF, and ID to the PC, so retiming must account for the decode/redirect path it exposes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
