{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r15s1.yaml; git status --short --untracked-files=all'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Use valid-tagged fetch bubbles\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The recent critical path runs from the imem stall signal through fetch,\n  register-file reads, ID branch resolution, and back into the fetch PC.\n  Fetch currently turns a stalled instruction into a NOP with a 32-bit mux,\n  so bus readiness affects decode and branch datapaths even though validity\n  already marks the instruction as unusable.\nhypothesis: |\n  Pass instruction bits directly from imem through IF/ID and represent fetch\n  bubbles with the existing valid bit instead of replacing the instruction\n  with a NOP. In ID, suppress decoded controls and branch redirects whenever\n  the incoming instruction is invalid; qualify branch hazard detection with\n  validity as well. This moves imemReady out of the instruction, register-file\n  address, and branch-compare paths while preserving the current pipeline\n  depth, retirement order, and stall behavior. The shorter readiness-to-PC\n  control path should improve Fmax without adding branch recovery cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Keep instruction data unmodified during fetch stalls and carry bubble state through the valid bit.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Suppress control effects and ID branch redirects for invalid fetch payloads.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Qualify branch dependency stalls with IF/ID validity when instruction bits can be arbitrary in a bubble.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
