{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r1s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r1s1\ntitle: Iterative divide execution\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The only measured timing path starts at the MEM/WB destination register and passes through forwarding and the EX ALU before reaching the data-memory address. The ALU currently contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder operators, which can dominate the datapath even when CoreMark is executing ordinary instructions.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU with a radix-2 iterative divider in EX. Hold fetch and decode while a divide is in progress, advance one quotient bit per cycle, and let the operation enter EX/MEM only when its architectural result is ready. This removes the deep divide network from the forwarding-to-ALU timing path; CoreMark is expected to pay little cycle cost because it rarely divides.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 15\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Remove combinational divide and remainder datapaths from the ordinary ALU while preserving their ALTOPS behavior and the other ALU operations.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Add a finite-latency iterative divider for signed and unsigned quotient/remainder operations, with correct divide-by-zero and signed-overflow results.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Add EX divide-busy stalling so younger instructions remain held until the divide result can advance, without repeatedly retiring older stages.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the EX divide-busy handshake to fetch/decode hazard control and preserve the existing memory-stall priority.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
