{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural|structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative divide execution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only measured timing path starts at the MEM/WB destination register and passes through forwarding and the EX ALU before reaching the data-memory address. The ALU currently contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder operators, which can dominate the datapath even when CoreMark is executing ordinary instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only measured timing path starts at the MEM/WB destination register and passes through forwarding and the EX ALU before reaching the data-memory address. The ALU currently contains combinational signed and unsigned division and remainder operators, which can dominate the datapath even when CoreMark is executing ordinary instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU with a radix-2 iterative divider in EX. Hold fetch and decode while a divide is in progress, advance one quotient bit per cycle, and let the operation enter EX/MEM only when its architectural result is ready. This removes the deep divide network from the forwarding-to-ALU timing path; CoreMark is expected to pay little cycle cost because it rarely divides.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational divide and remainder datapaths from the ordinary ALU while preserving their ALTOPS behavior and the other ALU operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a finite-latency iterative divider for signed and unsigned quotient/remainder operations, with correct divide-by-zero and signed-overflow results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add EX divide-busy stalling so younger instructions remain held until the divide result can advance, without repeatedly retiring older stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the EX divide-busy handshake to fetch/decode hazard control and preserve the existing\n…[21300 of 24372 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:11f3dacde0d30b37]…\negression\", \"fitness\": 117.17, \"delta_pct\": 3.32, \"vs_baseline\": 866.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 52.138, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4449669, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3244, \"ff\": 638, \"seeds\": [52.389, 52.138, 52.138], \"perturbations\": [[0, 52.389, 20], [1, 52.138, 21], [2, 52.138, 21]], \"lutram\": 40, \"bsram\": 4, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.alu_op_3_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_if/pc_9_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -14.088, \"arrival_ns\": 24.382, \"logic_cells\": 35, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_rf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_if\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 20, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T15:31:53.629838Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 (regression, +3.32%): Carry-select address splitting across EX/MEM still regressed CoreMark; parallel high-half sums, registered partials, and MEM selection add datapath cost, so narrower address adders need measured timing gains to pay off.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
