ppression, so weigh fetch-table cost against redirects actually saved.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, +0.16%): A parallel `rs1 + imm` effective-address path duplicates the 32-bit sum and adds an EX result mux; bypassing ALU selectors alone did not pay off here, so keep it only when measured timing gains outweigh datapath overhead.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -0.49%): For a single-issue CoreMark core, a synchronous register-file read plus IF/ID\n…[6334 of 9406 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:055bc6b37f551dbb]…\nits early redirect and `jal_early_redirect` pipeline suppression logic did not repay the sequential wrong-path redirect saved on CoreMark, so measure the dynamic call benefit before extending ID redirect control.\nhyp-20260930-003-r9s2 | Single-entry return target prediction | regression | delta 3.19 | - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r9s2 (regression, +3.19%): Even a one-entry return target cache keeps canonical-return decode, a fetch tag compare/target select, and 33 bits of carried prediction state in the front end, while suppressing EX redirects only on hits, so its always-paid cost still outweighed saved redirects on CoreMark.\nhyp-20260930-003-r10s2 | Compact return-target prediction | regression | delta -7.76 | - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r10s2 (regression, -7.76%): Partial-tag return caching still reconstructs a full fetch target and needs EX full-target mismatch correction for aliases, so narrower carried metadata alone did not repay the always-active JALR lookup and target-selection logic on CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
