{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r8s2.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-003-r8s2\ntitle: \\\"Resolve direct jumps in decode\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Conditional branches already redirect from ID, while direct JAL calls still wait for EX and squash the sequential instruction. CoreMark makes repeated calls, so removing that remaining redirect bubble may improve iterations per cycle. Earlier fetch-time prediction experiments regressed when they added target generation or predictor state to the front end.\nhypothesis: |\n  Resolve aligned, legal direct JAL targets in ID using the existing PC-plus-immediate target path, and redirect fetch on the same cycle the JAL enters ID/EX. Carry a one-bit early-resolution marker with that instruction so EX preserves its architectural next-PC and link result but suppresses the redundant redirect. Leave JALR and misaligned JAL handling on the existing EX path. This avoids a fetch-time target adder, predictor table, and wide prediction metadata while removing the sequential wrong-path bubble for direct calls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Add an ID/EX flag marking an aligned direct JAL already redirected from ID.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Issue an ID redirect for valid, legal, aligned direct JAL instructions using the existing PC-relative target calculation.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Suppress the EX redirect for a marked direct JAL while retaining jump result and next-PC behavior.\\\"\nEOF\ncat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r8s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
